## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about incremental data structures and Bloom filter trade-offs for query pruning in streaming text.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental suffix automaton with amortized O(1) update per character
- Bloom filters for O(1) query pruning per pattern, per batch
- Cache behavior: larger B reduces SAM update frequency but increases Bloom filter staleness and memory pressure
- Space: each block's Bloom filter costs O(B) bits; false positives introduce extra SAM verifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Extend SAM with a block of text
function extendSAM(sam, block) {
  for (const char of block) {
    sam.extend(char);
  }
}

// Build Bloom filter for a block's substrings
function buildBlockBloom(block, size) {
  const bloom = new BloomFilter(size, 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < block.length; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= block.length - i; len++) {
      bloom.add(block.slice(i, i + len));
    }
  }
  return bloom;
}

// Query patterns against accumulated text
function queryPatterns(sam, bloomFilters, patterns) {
  return patterns.map(p => {
    const mightMatch = bloomFilters.every(bf => bf.test(p));
    return mightMatch && sam.contains(p);
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of amortized batching, range updates, and exact coverage checks on a line, common in streaming analytics and scheduling systems.

## Key Concepts
- Coordinate compression, segment tree with range updates, global min check, amortized batching, space efficiency.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SegTree {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.min=Array(4*n).fill(0); this.cover=Array(4*n).fill(0); this.lazy=Array(4*n).fill(0); }
  _apply(i,delta){ this.cover[i]+=delta; this.min[i]+=delta; this.lazy[i]+=delta; }
  _push(i){ if(this.lazy[i]!==0){ this._apply(i*2,this.lazy[i]); this._apply(i*2+1,this.lazy[i]); this.lazy[i]=0; } }
  update(qL,qR,delta,i=1,l=0,r=this.n-1){ if(qR<l||r<qL) return; if(qL<=l&&r<=qR){ this._apply(i,delta); return; } this._push(i); const m=(l+r)>>1; this.update(qL,qR,delta,i*2,l,m); this.update(qL,qR,delta,i*2+1,m+1,r); this.min[i]=Math.min(this.min[i*2],this.min[i*2+1]); }
  fullCovered(){ return this.min[1]>0; }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle intervals with dynamic endpoints or non-integer endpoints?
- How would you adapt to non-uniform batch sizes or streaming deletions without prior insertions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design incremental geometry data structures, reason about dominance, and assess batch-update trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Pareto frontier maintenance
- Batch incremental updates
- Monotone frontier properties (x increasing, y decreasing)
- Trade-offs: update time vs rebuild cost

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch: merge batch into frontier
function insertBatch(frontier, batch){
  batch.sort((a,b)=> a.x-b.x);
  // prune intra-batch dominated
  let filtered=[]; let maxY=-Infinity;
  for(let p of batch){ if(p.y>maxY){ filtered.push(p); maxY=p.y; } }
  // merge with frontier (frontier: sorted by x, y strictly decreasing)
  let i=0,j=0; let newFrontier=[];
  while(i<frontier.length || j<filtered.length){
    let a = frontier[i], b = filtered[j];
    if(!a) { newFrontier.push(...filtered.slice(j)); break; }
    if(!b) { newFrontier.push(...frontier.slice(i)); break; }
    if(a.x < b.x){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; }
    else if(b.x < a.x){ newFrontier.push(b); j++; }
    else { // equal x, keep larger y
      if(a.y > b.y){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; j++; }
      else { newFrontier.push(b); i++; j++; }
    }
  }
  // optional prune to maintain y-decreasing property
  return pruneFrontier(newFrontier);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity-analysis: maintaining LIS under online batched updates tests block-based design, amortized reasoning, and practical trade-offs beyond typical path/graph problems.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) dynamics
- sqrt-decomposition / block partitioning
- per-block LIS profiles and cross-block composition
- amortized cost vs full recomputation
- practical optimizations and memory bounds

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple block LIS recompute (tails-based)
function computeBlockLIS(block) {
  const tails = [];
  for (const x of block) {
    let i = 0, j = tails.length;
    while (i < j) {
      const m = (i + j) >> 1;
      if (tails[m] < x) i = m + 1; else j = m;
    }
    tails[i] = x;
  }
  return tails;
}

// Placeholder for merging block profiles into global LIS length
function mergeBlocks(blocks) {
  let tails = [];
  for (const b of blocks) {
    // apply b.profile to current tails (conceptual)
    tails = applyProfile(tails, b.profile);
  }
  return tails.length;
}
```

